  "image_name": "00e1900a1e76c9da7eea75d48d92a653.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how fibroblast growth factor receptors (FGFRs) are activated, signal inside cells, are trafficked, and become altered in cancer. Overall, it shows that ligand binding at the plasma membrane drives FGFR dimerization and phosphorylation, which then triggers several downstream pathways, including PLCγ, RAS–ERK, and PI3K–AKT signaling. A structural inset highlights the kinase domain of FGFR, with key regions such as the N and C lobes, activation loop, hinge, and αC helix, and marks several mutation hotspots that appear to affect receptor activation.\n\nThe figure also emphasizes receptor trafficking after activation. Activated FGFRs are internalized through endocytosis into early endosomes, where they may either recycle back to the cell surface, move retrogradely toward the Golgi, or be sorted by ESCRT machinery into late endosomes for lysosomal degradation. This suggests that signaling strength and duration depend not only on activation but also on intracellular routing.\n\nAt the bottom, the figure compares major oncogenic FGFR alterations. These include gene fusions involving FGFR1, FGFR2, or FGFR3, activating point mutations in different FGFR family members, and amplification or overexpression. Together, the panels convey that cancer can exploit FGFR signaling through multiple molecular mechanisms.",
